{"id": "M2-c187", "lesson": "L13", "front": "For the central quotient, where is the balanced bound smallest?", "back": "For delta > 0 and M_3 > 0 (Lesson L13, Section 4), the bound (M_3/6) h^2 + delta/h, with M_3 = max |f'''| on [x - h_0, x + h_0], has derivative (M_3/3) h - delta/h^2, which vanishes at h* = (3 delta/M_3)^(1/3), the minimizer, provided h* <= h_0 (otherwise the smallest value on (0, h_0] is at h_0). The derivation is the same calculation as Corollary 4.1.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "M2-c234", "lesson": "L15", "front": "In Lab 1, what are f(1/k, 1/k) and g(1/k, 1/k) along the diagonal sequence?", "back": "g equals 0.500000 at every n, and f equals 1/(2n), printed as 0.050000000 at n = 10 and 0.000050000 at n = 10000. Lab 1 prints these values for n = 10, 100, 1000 and 10000 (Example 3.4).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1, sequence (1/n, 1/n) for n = 10, 100, 1000, 10000"}
